116
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:17,480
At the beginning of the 20th century, on the Gulf Coast in the U.S.

117
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:23,800
state of Texas, there was a hill where gas leakage was so noticeable that schoolboys

118
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:26,320
would sometimes set the hill on fire.

119
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:33,280
Patio Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil

120
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:34,580
below the gassy hill.

121
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,980
Oil wells weren't drilled back then.

122
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:39,620
They were essentially dug.

123
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:45,620
The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a

124
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:52,100
proper hole. Higgins had forecast oil at a thousand feet, a totally made -up figure.

125
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:57,620
Higgins subsequently hired a mining engineer, Captain Anthony Lucas.

126
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:03,140
After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill,

127
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:07,500
and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry.

128
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:15,340
In January 1901, at 1,020 feet, almost precisely the depth predicted by Higgins'

129
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:22,480
wild guess, the well roared and suddenly ejected mud and six tons of drilling pipe

130
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:25,580
out of the ground, terrifying those present.

131
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:31,700
For the next nine days until the well was capped, the well poured out more oil than

132
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:33,920
all the wells in America combined.

133
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:40,940
In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically

134
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:45,520
no industry. Cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy.

135
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:48,120
Higgins' well changed that.

136
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:54,820
The boom made some prospectors millionaires, but the sudden surplus of petroleum was

137
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:56,920
not entirely a blessing for Texas.

138
00:12:56,920 --> 00:13:03,800
In the 1930s, prices crashed to the point that, in some parts of the country, oil

139
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,080
was cheaper than water.

140
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:10,540
That would become a familiar pattern of the boom or bust Texas economy.

141
