153
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:05,380
Most people dislike their jobs.

154
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:09,400
It's an astonishing but statistical fact.

155
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:15,960
primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many

156
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:17,880
believe they have terrible managers.

157
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:23,620
Few describe their managers as malicious or manipulative, though.

158
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:27,280
While those types certainly exist, they are a minority.

159
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:32,380
The majority of managers seemingly just don't know any better.

160
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:36,300
They're often emulating bad managers they've had in the past.

161
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:41,240
It's likely they've never read a management book or attended a management course.

162
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:47,480
They might not have even reflected on what good management looks like and how it

163
00:15:47,480 --> 00:15:50,100
would influence their own management style.

164
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:53,760
The researchers interviewed employees about their managers,

165
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:59,040
beginning with a question about the worst manager they had ever had.

166
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:04,200
From this, the researchers came up with four main causes of why some managers are

167
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:07,440
perceived as being simply awful at their jobs.

168
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:13,960
The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor

169
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:20,080
management practices. It was specifically stressful work environments, minimal

170
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:25,600
training and a lack of accountability that were found to be most blameworthy.

171
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:30,820
Often, manager's superiors can effectively encourage a manager's distasteful

172
00:16:30,820 --> 00:16:34,240
behaviour when they fail to discipline the person's wrongdoings.

173
00:16:35,180 --> 00:16:38,580
Such workplaces are sometimes described as toxic.

174
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:44,100
The second cause was attributed to the manager's characteristics.

175
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:49,460
Those deemed to be most destructive were odd people, those without drive,

176
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:54,700
those who allow personal problems into the workplace and those with an unpleasant

177
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:57,240
temperament or personality in general.

178
00:16:58,220 --> 00:17:02,300
The third cause of poor management was associated with a deficiency of

179
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:08,420
qualifications. Not so much the formal variety one obtains from a university, but

180
00:17:08,420 --> 00:17:12,840
the informal variety that comes from credible work experience and professional

181
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:18,800
accomplishments. The fourth cause concerned managers who've been promoted for

182
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:20,860
reasons other than potential.

183
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:26,460
One reason in particular why these people had been promoted was that they had been

184
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:27,720
around the longest.

185
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:33,760
It wasn't their skill set or other merits that got them the job, it was their

186
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:40,340
tenure. A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of

187
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:47,100
employees. The researchers didn't ask senior leaders what they thought of their

188
00:17:47,100 --> 00:17:48,120
frontline managers.

189
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:54,460
It's quite possible they're content with how the individuals they promoted are now

190
00:17:54,460 --> 00:17:59,380
performing. Merely ignorant of the damage they're actually causing.

191
00:18:00,180 --> 00:18:05,620
Which might explain why, as the researchers conclude, those same middle managers are

192
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:09,400
usually unaware that they are a bad manager.

193
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:42,880
